  "reasoning": "The patient presents with persistent fatigue, altered taste, a scratchy throat, muscle pain, fever (described as heat), and occasional full-body cramps. These symptoms collectively point toward a systemic viral infection. Among the available diagnoses, Dengue is the strongest match. Dengue is classically characterized by high fever (heat), severe muscle and joint pain/cramps (often referred to as 'breakbone fever'), profound fatigue, and can frequently include throat irritation and taste disturbances. While conditions like the common cold share throat and taste symptoms, they typically lack the severe systemic muscle pain and cramping described here. Malaria emphasizes chills, sweating, and headaches, while Typhoid focuses more on gastrointestinal symptoms. Therefore, the combination of fever, significant myalgia, cramps, and fatigue aligns best with Dengue.",
  "final_answer": "[DIAGNOSIS]dengue[/DIAGNOSIS]"
}
